    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nThe pickle code generated by `ParseTreeTransforms` takes `state` under `CRITICAL_SECTION(self)`, but computes `use_setstate` after the critical section ends:\n\n```cython\nwith CRITICAL_SECTION(self):\n    state = (self.i, self.o)\n    _dict = getattr(self, '__dict__', None)\nif _dict is not None and _dict:\n    ...\nelse:\n    use_setstate = self.o is not None   # unlocked read of self.o\n```\n\nThe generated property setter for `cdef public object o` writes `self->o` inside a critical section. A concurrent `pickle.dumps(obj)` therefore races with `obj.o = ...`. ThreadSanitizer reports this on the existing `tests/run/threadsafe_generated.pyx::test_properties`. That test has no `threads` tag, so the default sanitizer job (`tag:threads`) never runs it; only `full_sanitizers` does.\n\nThe practical impact is small: it is a pointer compare, and both return paths unpickle correctly. But `use_setstate` can disagree with the snapshot in `state`, the read is a formal data race, and it adds noise to TSAN runs of the test that is meant to show generated code is thread-safe.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour\n\nOn master 7b3aab3, with a CPython main build (8c3b566) configured with `--disable-gil --with-thread-sanitizer` (clang 20):\n\n```\nPYTHON_GIL=0 CFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=thread\" TSAN_OPTIONS=\"log_path=san_log\" \\\n  python runtests.py --no-refnanny --backends=c,cpp threadsafe_generated\ngrep -c \"WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race\" san_log.*\n```\n\nEvery run reports 4 races (10 of 10 runs; 2 per backend):\n\n```\nWARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race\n  Read of size 8 by thread T3:\n    #0 __pyx_pw_20threadsafe_generated_1C_1__reduce_cython__ threadsafe_generated.c:3897\n    ...\n  Previous write of size 8 by thread T5:\n    #0 __pyx_setprop_20threadsafe_generated_1C_o threadsafe_generated.c:3519\n```\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nNo TSAN reports. Moving the `use_setstate = <any_notnone_members>` assignment into the `with CRITICAL_SECTION(self):` block removes all of them: 0 races in 10 of 10 runs. The pickle, reduce, dataclass, threadsafe, critical_section and freethreading tests still pass on 3.14t (c and cpp). Happy to open a PR with that change.\n\n### Environment\n\nLinux aarch64 (GH200 / Neoverse-V2), free-threaded CPython main with TSAN, clang 20.1.8. The race itself is not architecture-specific.\n\n### Additional context\n\n`Tools/examine_sanitizer_logs.py` skips `WARNING: ThreadSanitizer` lines, so TSAN reports in the uploaded logs never fail the sanitizer job. I'm not sure that is intended.\n",
